Read `Review.cpp` (7 lines, single class). It's not fine — the issues are all at the interface level, since the bodies are comment stubs:

```cpp
class Report {
public:
  void parse(const std::string& raw)   { /* tokenize and validate */ }
  void render(std::ostream& out)       { /* markdown formatting */ }
  void save(const std::string& path)   { /* write to disk */ }
  void load(const std::string& path)   { /* read from disk */ }
};
```

**1. SRP violation — one class, four responsibilities.** `parse` (tokenize/validate), `render` (presentation), and `save`/`load` (persistence) each change for different reasons: a new input format touches `parse`, a markdown→HTML switch touches `render`, moving storage to a DB or S3 touches `save`/`load`. None of that forces the others to change, so they belong in separate types.

**2. No state — it's a bag of functions, not an object.** The class has zero data members. Whatever `parse` produces (the parsed model) has no visible home, and `render`/`save` have nothing declared to render or save. As written the methods don't even cohere around shared state; this reads as either a missing representation (incomplete design) or four free functions that should live in separate namespaces/facades.

**3. ISP — every consumer sees everything.** A client that only renders must depend on `save`/`load`; one that only loads gets parsing and rendering for free. There's no way to depend on a single responsibility, so the class is a god-class seed: any new concern (export, diffing, caching) will just be bolted onto it.

**4. Inconsistent I/O abstraction.** `render(std::ostream&)` takes a stream — composable and testable — while `save(const std::string& path)` / `load(...)` take raw filesystem paths, hard-coding "disk" into the class and making persistence unmockable in tests. Pick one level: streams (or an injected backend) for both.

**5. Unobservable failure.** `parse` is documented as validating but returns `void`, so a caller can't distinguish success from a rejected input; same for `load`. At minimum these need error reporting (return status, exceptions, or out-params).

Caveat: since the bodies are placeholders, I'm judging the class's shape, not its implementation. The fix direction is to split along responsibility — e.g. a parsed-report value type, a parser, a renderer over `std::ostream`, and an injected storage backend — rather than one `Report` doing it all.

